I'm thinking that Marco hasn't stayed ashore since he was a boy, just living on board between voyages . So, Marco is really, really into Lucia. Quietly, he doesn't mention any of it. For a long time, Maria and Lucia's relationship is secret from Matteo and Marco (their husbands). When the husbands come home, Lucia and Matteo are all over each other. Marco and Maria have quiet company together. Maria is quietly jealous until she can learn some stratigies about it. Eventually, Maria notices that Marco is attracted to Lucia, something he probably doesn't realize himself. Maybe he seeks sex when Matteo and Lucia's noises can be heard. Maybe Maria straigt up tells him "We've never shared passion like that. I don't care if you take mistresses, just don't get the pregnant." I'm not sure how or when Lucia would invite Marco to her and Matteo's bed yet. 
I actually think you've stumbled onto something that makes these four relationships much richer if you keep the focus on emotional honesty rather than sexual logistics.
One thing that stands out is that Marco and Maria are mirrors of each other.
    • Marco never really wanted to settle ashore. 
    • Maria never really wanted a husband. 
    • Both did what society expected. 
    • Neither blames the other. 
That creates a marriage that isn't passionate, but isn't unhappy either.
Marco living aboard ship
I love this detail.
If he left at 13 or 14 and simply...never really came home, then a ship is home.
He knows every creak of a deck better than the layout of a Venetian house.
When he finally buys a house after becoming captain, it isn't because he desperately wanted one. It's because that's what a respectable captain does.
Maria probably makes it into a home.
Marco still occasionally sleeps badly because the floor doesn't move.

Lucia is the first woman Marco actually falls for
This feels stronger if it's gradual.
At first, she's simply Matteo's wife.
She's funny.
She argues with him.
She isn't intimidated by captains.
She asks questions about sailing.
She laughs at his dry jokes.
Years pass.
Without realizing it, Marco starts lingering in the kitchen while she's cooking.
He volunteers to fetch things for her.
He notices when she's tired.
He doesn't think,
"I'm in love."
He thinks,
"Lucia is wonderful."
Everyone except Marco notices where this is heading.

Maria notices first
This is my favorite part of your idea.
Maria has spent years studying people because she's had to hide part of herself.
She's very good at spotting attraction.
One day Marco is talking about Lucia.
Maybe it's something completely innocent.
"Lucia likes figs."
Maria smiles.
"Since when do you know what fruit she likes?"
Marco pauses.
"...I don't know."
Maria realizes before he does.

The conversation
I would make it much quieter than permission to "take mistresses."
Instead, Maria could frame it around what they've never had.
Something like:
"Marco... we've always been companions."
"Yes."
"Good companions."
"I'd like to think so."
"But we've never looked at each other the way Matteo looks at Lucia."
...
"You look at someone that way."
Marco's first instinct would probably be denial.
Not because he's ashamed, but because the thought has literally never occurred to him.

Marco's reaction
He's horrified.
Not because Lucia is married.
Because she's Matteo's wife.
Matteo is probably one of the people he trusts most.
His response is almost certainly:
"Then nothing can happen."
Problem solved.
He'll bury it.
He's been burying feelings his whole life.

Maria's perspective
Maria is interesting because she's simultaneously jealous and understanding.
She loves Lucia.
She notices Marco loves Lucia too.
Her jealousy isn't:
"Lucia will leave me."
It's more:
"She has something with Matteo I'll never have.
And now Marco might have something with her that I don't."
That's a much more nuanced emotion.
She isn't jealous of Marco.
She's jealous that Lucia seems able to inspire deep romantic love in everyone around her.

How Marco ends up in their relationship
I don't think Lucia should be the first person to suggest it.
That puts all the emotional labor on her.
Instead, I imagine it unfolding over years as trust deepens. By then, Lucia and Maria have already revealed their relationship to their husbands, Matteo has accepted it, and Marco has quietly buried his own feelings.
Eventually, Lucia realizes something she's somehow missed because Marco has never acted on it: he has loved her quietly for a very long time.
What would likely matter to Lucia isn't that Marco desires her—it's how he's handled that desire. He has never pressured her, flirted behind Matteo's back, or tried to come between anyone. He's simply remained kind and dependable.
That restraint is what makes him feel safe.
When the four of them finally talk openly, it becomes less about changing the structure of their relationships and more about recognizing what's already true. They already function as a family. They're already deeply devoted to one another. The conversation is about whether there's room to acknowledge one more bond that has been quietly growing all along.
That feels very consistent with the personalities you've described. None of these characters seem impulsive. They all appear to value loyalty, honesty, and consent. If Marco eventually becomes part of the romantic relationship, it would be because everyone involved has had time to understand their own feelings and chooses it together—not because of a single dramatic moment.
I also think that pacing suits your overall story. The emotional payoff isn't "they all end up in bed." It's that four people, each constrained in different ways by the expectations of fifteenth-century Venice, slowly build a household where each of them is finally seen and accepted for who they are. That's a much rarer and, in my opinion, more memorable story than a romance that hinges on immediate attraction alone.
