The house fire is a fantastic catalyst
I especially like this:
Marco invited Lucia and Matteo to stay with him after their house burns down.
That tells us a lot about Marco.
He isn't inviting Lucia because he secretly wants her.
He's inviting his first mate (or trusted crewman) and his wife because that's what a good captain does.
From Marco's perspective, it's probably as simple as:
"We've got two spare rooms. Stay until you're back on your feet."
No ulterior motive.

Living together changes everything
This is something that's hard to get in 15th-century Venice otherwise.
Normally, they'd only see each other occasionally.
Instead, now they're sharing everyday life.
Lucia sees Marco:
    • fixing furniture 
    • bringing fish home 
    • reading charts 
    • patiently teaching neighborhood boys knots 
    • falling asleep in a chair after a voyage 
Marco sees Lucia:
    • singing while cooking 
    • caring for Maria 
    • laughing with Matteo 
    • comforting people 
    • making the house feel warm 
Those ordinary moments are exactly how love often grows.

Maria and Lucia
I think these two probably fall in love first because they're together every day while the men are away at sea.
That also makes Maria's jealousy believable.
She already worries:
"When Matteo comes home, Lucia lights up."
Maria has to learn that loving more than one person doesn't necessarily diminish existing love. That's an emotional journey, not an overnight realization.

Marco and Matteo
One thing I really enjoy is imagining Matteo as the least surprised person in the room.
Imagine the reveal.
Marco finally admits:
"...I've loved Lucia for years."
Matteo blinks.
"...Marco."
"...Yes?"
"I've known."
Marco freezes.
"You...what?"
"You've looked at her like she's sunrise for about four years."
Lucia starts laughing.
Maria buries her face in her hands.
Marco is absolutely mortified.
It fits with the Marco you've described: perceptive about ships and people in practical matters, but almost oblivious to his own emotions.

Marco and Maria "coming out"
I also think this scene has the potential to be quietly funny because it lacks any sense of betrayal.
Maria says:
"I love Lucia."
Marco nods slowly.
"...I know."
"You knew?"
"I thought so."
A beat.
Marco sighs.
"...I think I'm in love with Lucia too."
Maria stares.
"...Marco."
"...Yes?"
"You are catastrophically bad at noticing your own feelings."

Why Lucia eventually invites Marco
I think the invitation should come after Lucia has watched Marco consistently choose their happiness over his own.
For years he has:
    • never flirted 
    • never hinted 
    • never asked for anything 
    • continued treating Matteo with complete respect 
    • continued supporting Maria 
    • quietly accepted that Lucia was "off limits" 
From Lucia's perspective, that's incredibly moving. She realizes that Marco's love has never been possessive. He's been content simply to have her in his life, even if only as family.
That realization changes the question from, "Should Marco join us?" to, "Why are we excluding someone who has already been acting like part of this family all along?"
That feels like a much stronger emotional turning point than an invitation based solely on attraction.
Overall, I think what makes this household compelling isn't the polycule itself—it's that every relationship serves a different emotional need. Marco and Maria provide stability and mutual respect. Matteo and Lucia share obvious passion and affection. Maria and Lucia discover a romantic connection that neither expected. Marco and Lucia grow into a quiet, deeply earned love. And Marco and Matteo remain brothers-in-arms, with enough trust that neither sees the other's love for Lucia as a threat. The result is a family that feels built over years rather than assembled all at once.






